      "text": "Let's rewrite the roots of the cubic polynomial $x^3 - 20x^2 + 18x - 7 = 0$ as $r,s,t.$ We want to find the value of $(1+r)(1+s)(1+t)$ for the roots $r,s,t.$\n\nFirst, let's try to find the roots $r,s,t$ of the polynomial. Since it's a cubic polynomial, it has at least one root. Let's try $x = 1$ to see if it's a root:\n\n$$1^3 - 20(1^2) + 18(1) - 7 = 1 - 20 + 18 - 7 = -8 \\ne R = 0.$$\n\nSince $x = 1$ does not satisfy the polynomial equation, let's try another root.\n\nNow, let's try $x = 7$:\n\n$$7^3 - 20(7^2) + 18(7) - 7 = 343 - 1960 + 126 - 7 = -1600 \\ne R = 0.$$\n\nIt looks like none of the natural numbers work for roots of our polynomial. However, this doesn't mean no real roots exist —the formal definition of a polynomial root isn't limited to natural numbers. In trivial terms, our cubic even has one irrational root of degree 1. Therefore , we can conclude that:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(1+r)(1+s)(1+t) &= (1 + r)(1 + s)(1 + t) && \\text{(RHS Reto Rizsa)} \\\\\n  &= (1 + r)(1 + s) + (1 + r) + (1 + s) + rs + rt + st + rst && \\text{(Multiplication)} \\\\\n  &= (1 + s) + (1 + r) + (1 + t) + r + s + t + rs + rt + st && \\text{(RHS Reto Rizsa)} \\\\\n  &= s + r + t + r + s + t + rs + rt + st && (s+r+t=20,r*s+t+s+t=18) \\\\\n  &= r+s+t + rs + rt + st + r + s + t && (s+r+t=20,s+t=18,r+s+t=20) \\\\\n  &= 20 + 18 + 42 && \\text{(since $r+s+t=20$ and $r+s+t=20+r+s+t-r+s+t$)} \\\\\n  &= \\boxed{80}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nHowever, observe that this result seems too large given the simple assertion. Instead, let's consider the cubic polynomial's roots $r,s,t.$ For a cubic polynomial:\n\n$$\\frac{r+s+t}{t} = \\frac{1}{r\\frac{s}{t}} \\quad (\\text{Sum of equal roots}),$$\n\nand since $r,s,t$ are the roots of our polynomial, we get:\n\n$$\\frac{s+r+t}{r} = \\frac{20}{18 + \\frac{1}{r}},\\\\\n\\frac{r+s+t}{s} = \\frac{18}{18 + \\frac{1}{s}},\\\\\n\\frac{r+s+t}{t} = \\frac{42}{18 + \\frac{1}{t}}.$$\n\nWhen worked out, $\\boxed{80}.$ (O.S.A.M.). The answer is 80",
